
1. Implementation of Avatar
    Packages/Classes: My avatar package contains two files, one for the avatar itself and one for an enum i
                      used inside the avatar class (movementState).
                      I decided to add an enum to avoid having to keep multiple boolean fields (isRunning,
                      isStanding, isJumping) it keeps the state field cleaner.

    Changes of state: I added a field inside the avatar of type movementState, it gets updated as the avatar
                      moves to always reflect the state it's in. Every time the movementState gets updated
                      renderer().setRenderable is used to change the set of images the avatar is rendered with
                      (creating the movement animation).

    Energy display:   To update the percentage of energy (the number itself not the rendering) i created 2
                      function inside the avatar - increaseEnergy, reduceEnergy, the avatar calls them as he
                      runs / jumps / eats a fruit or just relaxes.

                      I added a separate class inside pepse.world that is responsible for rendering the
                      percentage of energy the avatar has at any time, it uses a game object and a text
                      renderable. This EnergyTextDisplay class has an update method that can be used to change
                      the number this renderable shows.

                      The avatar has a field to hold a callback method, when we create the energy
                      displayer in the manager we set the callback in the avatar to be the update function of
                      this energyDisplay object. Inside the avatar anytime we call a method to increase or
                      decrease the energy we also use this callback to update the display.

                      Using a callback allows the avatar to notify the display only when the energy value
                      changes, this design avoids unnecessary updates every frame.

                      In all, the avatar is responsible only for managing the energy value while the visual
                      representation is handled by the EnergyTextDisplay, this separation follows the single
                      responsibility principle and allows the energy display to be changed without changing
                      the avatar's logic.

2. Trees package
    I divided the trees package into 3 classes.
    Flora:  The flora class acts as a factory for creating trees leaves and fruits in a given range (using its
            createInRange method. it groups together the creation of all tress an fruit to a single function
            making it cleaner and simpler.

    Tree:   The sole purpose of this class is to generate a list of object that will represent a tree.
            The tree class is a class with only static methods, and only one is public - createTree.
            This method creates a trunk, then add leaves and fruit on it and returns a list of game objects to
            be added to the collection. The trunk and leaves are represented using blocks, only the fruits are
            a separate gameObject. (because they are the only one with a specific more special task)

    Fruit: The fruit class extends game object, each fruit has a boolean field 'available' that represent if
           it has been eaten in the last cycle or if it is available for the avatar to eat. When a fruit
           collides with an object with the AvatarTag it becomes unavailable and transparent (the renderer is
           set to null) for a whole cycle.
           I used a schedule task to handle fruit respawning after a full cycle, this allows time based
           behavior to be handled without using manual time tracking in update functions. After a full cycle
           has passed since creating the schedule it calls 'makeAvailable' that reset the fruit to being
           visible and eatable again.



